<node id="1172666" title="why are hex values not numbers?" created="2016-09-26 16:36:16" updated="2016-09-26 16:36:16" _doctext_mode="lenient"><type id="115">perlquestion</type>
<author id="438862">perl-diddler</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">Why are hex values not interpreted as numbers:

&lt;code&gt;
printf "dec(%d) hex(%x)", "65", "0x41";
Argument "0x41" isn't numeric in printf at -e line 2.
dec(65) hex(0)

# vs. shell:
&gt; printf "dec(%d) hex(0x%08x)\n" "65" "0x41"
dec(65) hex(0x00000041)

&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
I know about &lt;i&gt;hex&lt;/i&gt;, that doesn't explain why perl shouldn't be able to DWIM, no?
&lt;p&gt;
p.s. maybe the latest perl should allow 
"&lt;b&gt;0x41"-&gt;$*&lt;/b&gt; ? (tongue-in-cheek)

</doctext>
